Title:
DCOM Explained: How Attackers Turn a Windows Feature into a Lateral Movement Tool

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Walkthrough of how Windows DCOM works internally and how attackers abuse it for lateral movement using valid credentials.  
- It explains the DCOM activation path (COM APIs → RPCSS Endpoint Mapper on TCP 135 → SCM → dynamic high RPC port) and why it enables “fileless” remote execution via trusted COM servers.  
- A concrete attack flow is shown: credentials dumped from LSASS on one host are used to remotely instantiate `MMC20.Application` (`mmc.exe -Embedding`) and execute commands that spawn a child `powershell.exe`.  
- The defensive focus is on correlating Security Event ID 4624 (Type 3 logon) with Sysmon network telemetry (port 135) and suspicious process lineage (`svchost.exe -k DcomLaunch` → `mmc.exe`/other COM host → `cmd.exe`/`powershell.exe`).  
- It provides investigation pivots and hardening guidance (restrict DCOM remote activation, block peer-to-peer RPC/135, reduce credential reuse with LAPS, and protect LSASS).  
- Useful primarily for Blue Team/SOC and detection engineers, but also helps red teamers understand the observable footprint of DCOM-based movement.

Technical Focus:
- DCOM/COM architecture (CLSID, `CoCreateInstanceEx`, `HKCR\CLSID`)
- RPCSS Endpoint Mapper and RPC over TCP 135 + dynamic high ports
- DCOM Launch service (`svchost.exe -k DcomLaunch`) and SCM (`services.exe`) behavior
- Telemetry correlation: Security 4624 + Sysmon Event IDs 1 and 3
- Process tree anomalies (`mmc.exe -Embedding` spawning PowerShell/CMD)
- Mitre ATT&CK mapping (T1021.003 DCOM)

Use Cases:
- Build detections for DCOM lateral movement using log correlation (4624 + Sysmon netconn + process lineage)
- Triage DCOM alerts by validating RPC/135 source, account context, and spawned COM host processes
- Harden endpoints by restricting remote DCOM activation rights and segmenting RPC traffic
- Threat hunting for “svchost(DcomLaunch) → COM host → interpreter” execution chains
- Validate defensive coverage for fileless lateral movement techniques

Keywords:
DCOM, COM, lateral movement, RPCSS, Endpoint Mapper, TCP 135, dynamic RPC ports, CoCreateInstanceEx, CLSID, HKCR\\CLSID, services.exe, svchost.exe, DcomLaunch, MMC20.Application, mmc.exe -Embedding, ExecuteShellCommand, Sysmon Event ID 1, Sysmon Event ID 3, Security Event ID 4624, Logon Type 3, NTLM, LSASS credential dumping, T1021.003, PowerShell, LAPS, MachineLaunchRestriction, dcomcnfg